  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given that $r(t)=\u27e82\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,{t}^{2}-t\u27e9,$ ${r}^{\u2033}(t)=$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the second derivative of a vector-valued function, we find the second derivatives of the components.\n\n- For $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t \\\\ {x}^{\u2033}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y(t)={t}^{2}-t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}(t) & =2t-1 \\\\ {y}^{\u2033}(t) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{\u2033}(t) & =\u27e8{x}^{\u2033}(t),\\,{y}^{\u2033}(t)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,\\,2\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
